WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:07.950
It didn't take long, but Pokemon Go has became
a global sensation only days after it was

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:12.670
released. Fans are wild for the augmented-reality
game, which sends Pokemon trainers out into

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:15.530
their neighborhoods and communities on a quest
to catch 'em all.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:25.120
(intense Pokemon guy singing)

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:28.539
And naturally, the massive popularity of the
app means we've already learned plenty of

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:33.210
tips and tricks. Here's a list of what all
you trainers should watch out for as you play...as

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:37.329
well as a few real-life issues that trainers
have encountered along the way.

00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:38.899
Don't Waste Your Stuff

00:00:38.899 --> 00:00:43.859
The first lesson we learned when playing Pokémon
Go is that timed consumables run on real time,

00:00:43.859 --> 00:00:48.389
not in-game time. If you use one of these
items and the game crashes for an hour, you'll

00:00:48.389 --> 00:00:53.329
log back in to find your buff already expired.
Don't use an Incense, Lure Module, or Lucky

00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:56.859
Egg unless you have thirty minutes available
to make the most of it.

00:00:56.859 --> 00:00:58.370
Don't Trespass

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:03.489
Game developer Niantic isn't new to the augmented-reality
game. In their previous release, Ingress,

00:01:03.489 --> 00:01:07.500
users visit and claim local points of interest
for their team. Niantic re-used these same

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.200
points of interest when developing the locations
for Pokémon Go. So be smart! Don't risk the

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.039
wrath of property owners or authorities by
trying to reach these same locations in Pokémon

00:01:17.039 --> 00:01:17.520
Go.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.160
"Hey you! Get offa my property! Now!"

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:20.740
(blam)

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.880
Don't Miss the Intel

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:27.590
One major drawback of Pokémon Go is the lack
of a mapping tool. Some unofficial maps have

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:32.689
sprung up, but none are as complete or as
easy to use as the official map made for Ingress.

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:37.020
As we mentioned before, Niantic recycled its
Ingress portal locations for Pokémon Go.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.570
So do yourself a favor: download the game
and sign up for an account. Even if you don't

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:46.929
plan on playing, you can use the Ingress online
intel tool to easily find your nearest portals,

00:01:46.929 --> 00:01:48.450
which are also Gyms or Pokéstops.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:53.119
"Again I would like you all to come in, to
my Poke Stop restaurants, here in Mililani

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:57.579
Mauka as well as my Waipahu location. So thank
you very much, hope to see you at my Poke

00:01:57.579 --> 00:01:58.320
Stop restaurants."

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.920
And if you wind up at this dude's fish restaurant
in Hawaii, you've walked too far. Better check

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:04.289
that map again...

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:06.249
Don't catch and drive

00:02:06.249 --> 00:02:10.289
This should be obvious, but don't swipe away
with your Pokeball in the car. While you can

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:14.349
technically catch wild Pokemon at any speed,
is it really worth the risk?

00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:15.670
(crash)

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:21.390
You should only be driving to get to your
location, then get out and get some exercise.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.430
That's the point of the entire game, after
all.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:27.130
Well, that, and for Nintendo to earn a bunch
of money after the Wii U flopped.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.340
Ditch those extra Pidgeys

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.750
If you play enough, you'll probably have a
lot of extra Pidgeys or other low-level Pokemons

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:37.060
knocking around. Did you know that you can
exchange them for something better?

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:37.670
"Pika?"

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:42.310
If you transfer a Pokemon to Professor Willow,
he'll give you one species-specific candy

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:46.410
in exchange. You'll also get three candies
every time you catch a new Pokemon. And because

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:50.930
you use candies to power-up and evolve your
Pokemon, you can catch and transfer 19 Pidgeys

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:54.460
and have enough candy to evolve the twentieth
one all the way to Pidgeot.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:59.260
Of course, when we eat that much candy, we
don't evolve into anything. We just do this:

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.620
(Bart Simpson going crazy)

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.590
Don't Evolve Without a Lucky Egg

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:08.820
Speaking of evolution, you should always have
a Lucky Egg active before you evolve a Pokemon.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.370
Lucky Eggs are different from regular eggs,
and don't require an incubator. They give

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.750
double XP for 30 minutes. Ideally, you'll
want to have enough candy on hand to evolve

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:21.150
several Pokémon in a row. Go to your items
menu and select a Lucky Egg, then tap it on

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.210
your screen to activate it. Next, start evolving!

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:28.650
Of course when we eat lucky eggs, we don't
get any extra XP. We just do this:

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.920
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.370
you just saw. And leave us a comment to share
your tips for Pokemon Go...

